// A struct holding a parameter's borrow, built in a local, and a getter's
// result returned. The getter's signature is unannotated, so its result is
// tied to the borrow of the struct -- a local -- and may not be returned,
// although the field it reads is the caller's. Saying "the result comes from
// what H holds, not from the borrow of H" needs a lifetime on the struct type,
// whose spelling is still open.
//
// Verdict: refuse, ErrorEscape at the returned call.
// Basis: reflifefn.html (all unannotated borrows in a signature share one
// lifetime, so 'get''s result has the shortest of its inputs, '&h''s) --
// a reading of that rule. Rust agrees for the elided getter:
// 'fn get(&self) -> &R' ties the result to '&self', so 'h.get()' returned
// from a local 'h' is E0515 (by reading, not run); Rust accepts it only with 'impl<'a> H<'a> {
// fn get(&self) -> &'a R }'. An earlier analysis listed this refusal as
// wrong; the unannotated program is refused by the rule.
// Master d331baab: correct.

mod lifetime_mix_struct_getter_result_returned;

struct R { pub n i64; }

struct H {
  pub r &R;
  pub fn get(self &) &R { r; }
}

fn f(p &R) &R {
  imm h = new H(p);
  h.get();                   //~ ErrorEscape
}
